    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36-a1">

                <pb facs="0084" n="670" sameAs="frus:frus1949v07p2/pg670"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Annex—Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Recommendations</hi>
                </p>
                <p>This Department recommends that: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>The DRB should be instructed to broaden the principles which it has
                            adopted for the identification of excessive concentrations, in order to
                            insure that: <list>
                                <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                                <item>groups of companies related by horizontal ties are separated,
                                    wherever the economic power of the group is such as to
                                    constitute a threat to the development of competitive
                                    enterprise; and</item>
                                <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                                <item>when a single company consisting of a number of
                                    technologically unrelated lines constitutes a threat to the
                                    development of competitive enterprise because of the cumulative
                                    economic power of these lines, such company is designated as an
                                    excessive concentration.</item>
                            </list>
                        </item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>After such changes are made in the principles of implementation as are
                            necessitated by (1) above, the DRB should assist the HCLC and the FTC to assimilate and carry out the program
                            satisfactorily. It should then promptly withdraw from any further
                            interference in the decisions of these Japanese agencies, in accordance
                            with the requirements of NSC
                            13/2.</item>
                        <label>(3)</label>
                        <item>Any revisions in Public Law 54 should be carefully drafted in the
                            light of the need, not only for the prevention of restraints on
                            competition in any one field of industry, but also for the prevention of
                            the regrowth of the Zaibatsu form of control.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
            </div>
